          id: '101_4_8_1',
          question: 'Examine the role of Raja Ram Mohan Roy in Indian social reform.',
          answer: 'Raja Ram Mohan Roy (born 1772, Radhanagar, Bengal) is known as the \'father of the Indian Renaissance\' — a great patriot, scholar, and humanist who worked for the social, religious, intellectual, and political regeneration of India.\n\nIn 1814, Roy settled in Calcutta and dedicated his life to social and religious reform. He fought relentlessly against sati, polygamy, child marriage, female infanticide, and caste discrimination. He organised a movement against sati and helped William Bentinck pass the law banning it in 1829 — the first successful social movement against an age-old social evil.\n\nRoy was one of the earliest propagators of modern Western education. He was associated with the foundation of Hindu College in Calcutta (later Presidency College) and maintained at his own cost an English school in Calcutta. He also established a Vedanta College offering both Indian learning and Western social and physical science. He sent petitions to the government to adopt wider public education in English. He compiled a Bengali grammar and developed a modern Bengali prose style.\n\nRoy was a pioneer of Indian journalism, publishing journals in Bengali, Persian, Hindi, and English; Samvad Kaumudi was the most important journal. He was also a firm believer in internationalism, taking keen interest in international events and celebrating the success of the revolution in Spain in 1823 by hosting a public dinner.\n\nReligiously, Roy argued that the Vedas and Upanishads upheld monotheism. In 1829, he founded the Atmiya Sabha (later Brahmo Samaj), based on the twin pillars of rationalism and the philosophy of the Vedas, emphasising human dignity, criticising idolatry, and denouncing social evils like sati. He opposed the rigidity of the caste system as destructive to national unity. Rabindranath Tagore remarked: \'Rammohan was the only person in his time, in the whole world of men, to realise completely the significance of the Modern Age.\'', page: 252,
