          answer: 'The Charter Act of 1813 permitted missionaries to land in India in large numbers and establish modern English schools, laying \'the foundation of the well-organised modern educational system.\' It was the first parliamentary approval for propagation of Christianity in India.\n\nIn education, early missionaries pioneered both English and modern vernacular education. The Jesuits first established Christian institutions of learning in the sixteenth century. William Carey and the British Baptists pioneered modern education in North India; by 1818 there were 111 schools from Shimla to Delhi. Serampore College (1818) was the first Western-type postsecondary school. Alexander Duff\'s arrival in Calcutta in 1830 marked the beginning of English-language education that expanded throughout British India — English became the veritable lingua franca of India. Christians were pioneers in female education; in 1834 only 1 percent of Indian women could read and write, but by 1900 impressive numbers of schools and colleges had opened for women, including for tribal and untouchable communities.\n\nIn language, missionaries contributed significantly: Constanzio Beschi reformed Tamil alphabetical characters and produced a fourfold Tamil dictionary; Kellogg produced A Grammar of the Hindi Language (1893). William Carey and colleagues beginning in 1818 were the first to produce periodicals, journals, and a newspaper — the Friends of India, now the Statesman. Nehru acknowledges their contribution in allowing regional languages to emerge and blossom.\n\nIn social reform, Carey campaigned against infanticide from 1793; in 1802 infanticide was declared murder. By 1814, Ram Mohan Roy joined Carey\'s campaign against sati, and in 1829 Lord Bentinck prohibited it. Christians established CMC Ludhiana (1893) and CMC Vellore (1895), the first government-recognised medical colleges for women. Pandita Ramabai\'s Mukti Mission (1898) helped orphaned girls and abused women.', page: 268,
