          question: 'Examine the impact of Western education on Indian society.',
          answer: 'Western education transformed Indian society in fundamental ways as described in Lesson 15.\n\nPre-colonial India had Gurukulas and Pathasalas for Hindus, and Madarasas and Maktabs for Muslims. The British followed non-interference until 1813. The Charter Act of 1813 set apart one lakh rupees for education. The controversy between Orientalists (H.H. Wilson, H.T. Prinsep) who favoured Sanskrit and Persian, and Anglicists (W.W. Hunter, Sir Charles Trevelyan, Raja Ram Mohan Roy) who favoured English, was resolved by Macaulay\'s Minute (1835), which aimed to create persons \'Indians in blood and colour but English in taste, in opinion, in morals and in intellect.\' Wood\'s Despatch (1854) established universities at Calcutta, Bombay, and Madras and a graded network of schools. Various commissions—Hunter (1882), Sadler (1917–19), Hartog (1929), Radhakrishnan (1948–49), Kothari (1964–66)—progressively shaped the system.\n\nThe impact on society, described in Section 15.23, was far-reaching. The spread of Western education broke the intellectual monopoly of upper castes; equality of man came to be recognised; the Western concept of liberalism found support. Education contributed in three specific ways: it broke the Brahminical monopoly by opening doors to all classes; led to the emergence of professional classes who championed liberal reforms; and enabled occupational and social mobility.\n\nStudy of European sciences and literature paved the way for social and religious reform movements. Rule of law replaced customs; democratic idealism was opened to educated Indians—almost all nationalist leaders came from educated classes. Vernacular literature developed aided by printing presses. Westernisation altered society—breaking traditional joint family systems and bringing new food habits, dress, and entertainment.', page: 299,
